WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.855
&gt;&gt; Please welcome, Director of

00:00:06.856 --> 00:00:12.190
Data Science Microsoft
Research, Vani Mandava.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:17.250
&gt;&gt; Hello everyone, and welcome to

00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:21.270
these last two sessions
of Faculty Summit 2018.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.225
So in 2017, 57% of

00:00:28.226 --> 00:00:29.640
professional occupations in

00:00:29.641 --> 00:00:31.720
the United States
were held by women.

00:00:31.721 --> 00:00:34.485
Yet, only 17% of

00:00:34.486 --> 00:00:38.160
Fortune 500 CIOs positions
were held by women.

00:00:38.161 --> 00:00:39.910
In computing specifically,

00:00:39.911 --> 00:00:43.325
only 26% of jobs
were held by women.

00:00:43.326 --> 00:00:45.110
The numbers are even lower for

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:47.600
under-represented
groups Black and

00:00:47.601 --> 00:00:49.275
Hispanic men for example,

00:00:49.276 --> 00:00:51.995
hold only nine percent
of computing jobs,

00:00:51.996 --> 00:00:53.440
and the numbers for

00:00:53.441 --> 00:00:57.390
black and Hispanic women are
an abysmal four percent.

00:00:57.391 --> 00:01:00.860
As a result,
the technical products

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:02.150
and services that we use

00:01:02.151 --> 00:01:04.070
today had been created by

00:01:04.071 --> 00:01:08.215
a single large homogenous
group of people, men.

00:01:08.216 --> 00:01:10.700
Considering the impact
that technology

00:01:10.701 --> 00:01:12.720
has on our economy,

00:01:12.721 --> 00:01:14.675
our society, and our lives,

00:01:14.676 --> 00:01:18.555
it makes sense to pause
and consider the question,

00:01:18.556 --> 00:01:21.560
what if women and other
groups under-represented

00:01:21.561 --> 00:01:24.360
in technology we're at the table,

00:01:24.361 --> 00:01:28.490
designing and creating
technology alongside men?

00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.095
This bundle of computing
innovators will explore

00:01:33.096 --> 00:01:35.000
the exciting outcomes that are

00:01:35.001 --> 00:01:37.130
possible if diverse perspectives

00:01:37.131 --> 00:01:38.640
were brought to the table.

00:01:38.641 --> 00:01:42.855
It is my pleasure to
introduce Lucy Sanders,

00:01:42.856 --> 00:01:45.740
our esteemed moderator for
this distinguished panel.

00:01:45.741 --> 00:01:48.710
Lucy Sanders, the CEO
and co-founder of

00:01:48.711 --> 00:01:51.770
the National Center for Women
and Information Technology

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:53.050
and [inaudible].

00:01:53.051 --> 00:01:55.220
Lucy has extensive
industry background

00:01:55.221 --> 00:01:57.020
having worked in R and D,

00:01:57.021 --> 00:02:00.080
and executive VP positions
at AT and T Labs,

00:02:00.081 --> 00:02:03.755
Bell Labs and Avaya
Labs for over 20 years.

00:02:03.756 --> 00:02:05.690
Lucy specialized in systems that

00:02:05.691 --> 00:02:07.310
has software and solutions,

00:02:07.311 --> 00:02:08.900
and also hold six patents in

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:11.730
the area of communications
technology.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:13.250
Lucy is the recipient of

00:02:13.251 --> 00:02:16.820
the very prestigious Bell Labs
Fellow Award which is

00:02:16.821 --> 00:02:19.295
the highest technical
accomplishment

00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:21.060
bestowed at the company.

00:02:21.061 --> 00:02:22.820
Lucy currently serves as

00:02:22.821 --> 00:02:25.155
a trustee for the
Colorado School of Mines,

00:02:25.156 --> 00:02:27.040
The Center of
American Entrepreneurship

00:02:27.041 --> 00:02:28.550
in Washington DC and

00:02:28.551 --> 00:02:30.410
the International Computer
Science Institute

00:02:30.411 --> 00:02:32.690
at the University of
California Berkeley.

00:02:32.691 --> 00:02:34.880
She has served on
the information technology

00:02:34.881 --> 00:02:37.130
research and development
ecosystem commissioned for

00:02:37.131 --> 00:02:39.440
the national academies and the

00:02:39.441 --> 00:02:40.910
innovation Advisory Board for

00:02:40.911 --> 00:02:42.800
the US Department of Commerce.

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:44.390
On behalf of Microsoft,

00:02:44.391 --> 00:02:46.310
it is a great honor to welcome

00:02:46.311 --> 00:02:47.540
Lucy Sanders and

00:02:47.541 --> 00:02:51.360
our distinguished panel
for this session.

00:03:01.070 --> 00:03:04.875
&gt;&gt; Oh thanks Vani for
that really great welcome,

00:03:04.876 --> 00:03:08.870
and welcome to Jim and
Margaret and Abolade

00:03:08.871 --> 00:03:10.820
for coming today and sharing

00:03:10.821 --> 00:03:14.205
some insights around
diversity and innovation.

00:03:14.206 --> 00:03:16.550
As a community, those of us in

00:03:16.551 --> 00:03:18.930
computing and all of us
are I think in this room.

00:03:18.931 --> 00:03:22.445
We will talk about broadening
participation in computing,

00:03:22.446 --> 00:03:24.220
quite a bit when we get together,

00:03:24.221 --> 00:03:28.640
and often we'll talk about
it as a pipeline issue.

00:03:28.641 --> 00:03:32.325
We need to focus on the
computing educational pipeline,

00:03:32.326 --> 00:03:33.920
and that's true, we do.

00:03:33.921 --> 00:03:36.530
That's a very important
thing or we'll talk

00:03:36.531 --> 00:03:39.620
about it as being
the right thing to do,

00:03:39.621 --> 00:03:42.650
well-paying jobs for
a diverse group of people,

00:03:42.651 --> 00:03:45.235
a workforce issue and those
are all true as well.

00:03:45.236 --> 00:03:47.210
But today, we're not
going to talk about

00:03:47.211 --> 00:03:49.160
any of those important things.

00:03:49.161 --> 00:03:51.425
We're going to talk
today about the link

00:03:51.426 --> 00:03:54.415
between innovation and diversity.

00:03:54.416 --> 00:03:57.300
It's often a conversation
we don't stop and have,

00:03:57.301 --> 00:03:58.475
and when you think about,

00:03:58.476 --> 00:04:00.575
it it's the real reason
why we're in this game.

00:04:00.576 --> 00:04:02.350
It's not just a numbers game.

00:04:02.351 --> 00:04:05.390
We want to see what great
creative ideas

00:04:05.391 --> 00:04:06.560
would come to the table when

00:04:06.561 --> 00:04:08.835
diverse populations are there.

00:04:08.836 --> 00:04:11.805
So, the link between
innovation and diversity,

00:04:11.806 --> 00:04:13.630
between creativity and diversity,

00:04:13.631 --> 00:04:16.895
between great research
outcomes and diversity

00:04:16.896 --> 00:04:20.850
are really important things
that this panel will explore.

00:04:20.851 --> 00:04:22.550
Everybody on the panel today is

00:04:22.551 --> 00:04:25.030
a noted innovator and
his or her own right,

00:04:25.031 --> 00:04:27.920
and also somebody who cares
deeply about diversity and

00:04:27.921 --> 00:04:31.240
inclusion or my favorite
new word belonging.

00:04:31.241 --> 00:04:33.230
You know, where you are

00:04:33.231 --> 00:04:35.674
working and where
you are studying.

00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:39.200
So, it's a super important
topic because we

00:04:39.201 --> 00:04:40.780
know from research and Vani

00:04:40.781 --> 00:04:43.155
mentioned some of this
in her opening remarks,

00:04:43.156 --> 00:04:45.730
that diverse teams solve

00:04:45.731 --> 00:04:49.785
complex problems faster and
they get to a better answer.

00:04:49.786 --> 00:04:52.540
They are also have

00:04:52.541 --> 00:04:54.710
higher collective intelligence in

00:04:54.711 --> 00:04:56.720
problem solving as well.

00:04:56.721 --> 00:04:59.480
It's very important that
these diverse teams be very well

00:04:59.481 --> 00:05:01.820
managed because homogeneous teams

00:05:01.821 --> 00:05:03.500
are often easier to manage.

00:05:03.501 --> 00:05:05.060
But they don't necessarily get

00:05:05.061 --> 00:05:07.470
to the most innovative outcome.

00:05:07.471 --> 00:05:09.350
So, it's important that they be

00:05:09.351 --> 00:05:11.975
really well managed as well.

00:05:11.976 --> 00:05:14.975
So, I know these are
things we all care about

00:05:14.976 --> 00:05:18.320
and our panelists
care about it too.

00:05:18.321 --> 00:05:20.570
So we're going to start the
questioning with a little bit

00:05:20.571 --> 00:05:23.180
of storytelling here because

00:05:23.181 --> 00:05:25.850
I think it's important to
establish this link from

00:05:25.851 --> 00:05:28.745
their broad perspective
this as innovators,

00:05:28.746 --> 00:05:30.980
about the links
they've seen between

00:05:30.981 --> 00:05:33.595
technical innovation
and diversity.

00:05:33.596 --> 00:05:35.690
So, I'm going to ask each one.

00:05:35.691 --> 00:05:39.500
We'll go first to Margaret.
Thank you, Margaret.

00:05:39.501 --> 00:05:42.530
To talk about a technical project

00:05:42.531 --> 00:05:43.910
or team they were involved

00:05:43.911 --> 00:05:48.510
in that had particularly
innovative results.

00:05:48.511 --> 00:05:51.610
So the link between
innovation and diversity.

00:05:51.611 --> 00:05:53.200
So Margaret,

00:05:53.201 --> 00:05:55.850
tell us about your story and
we're going to geek out.

00:05:55.851 --> 00:05:57.200
So, we're going to
really be talking

00:05:57.201 --> 00:05:58.780
about technology
really geek out here.

00:05:58.781 --> 00:06:00.330
So, go ahead Margaret.

00:06:00.331 --> 00:06:03.175
&gt;&gt; Sure. Thanks. So, it's
wonderful to be here.

00:06:03.176 --> 00:06:07.470
I guess rather than think
about one zoomed in,

00:06:07.471 --> 00:06:09.740
I'm going to talk about an
overall culture that's been in

00:06:09.741 --> 00:06:12.470
my research group over
the 25 years now,

00:06:12.471 --> 00:06:14.095
nearly that I've
been at Princeton,

00:06:14.096 --> 00:06:17.215
and that is about projects
that are interdisciplinary,

00:06:17.216 --> 00:06:19.315
and so there's
a diversity of topic,

00:06:19.316 --> 00:06:21.770
a diversity of
skill sets that come in,

00:06:21.771 --> 00:06:23.480
and then a diversity
of backgrounds

00:06:23.481 --> 00:06:25.080
and genders and so forth as well.

00:06:25.081 --> 00:06:29.110
So, about a little
over 15 years ago,

00:06:29.111 --> 00:06:32.510
I started a project called
ZebraNet that was basically

00:06:32.511 --> 00:06:34.370
started because I a computer

00:06:34.371 --> 00:06:36.890
systems person who cared
about power efficiency.

00:06:36.891 --> 00:06:39.920
Got coupled with one of
my biology colleagues from

00:06:39.921 --> 00:06:41.570
across campus who wanted

00:06:41.571 --> 00:06:43.615
better wildlife tracking devices.

00:06:43.616 --> 00:06:47.390
We worked across that
intellectual gap to come up with

00:06:47.391 --> 00:06:49.640
designs that abided by

00:06:49.641 --> 00:06:51.860
their needs and
their desires to get better,

00:06:51.861 --> 00:06:54.920
fine-grained tracking
of zebras and abided

00:06:54.921 --> 00:06:57.620
by the engineering constraints

00:06:57.621 --> 00:06:59.495
to the problem which
was very tough,

00:06:59.496 --> 00:07:05.060
power constraints, and
GPS limitations, and so forth.

00:07:05.061 --> 00:07:08.420
Coupled in all of that was
a diversity of people as well,

00:07:08.421 --> 00:07:11.690
because you bring in people
with conservation experience,

00:07:11.691 --> 00:07:14.185
you bring in people with
deep engineering experience,

00:07:14.186 --> 00:07:17.540
male, female, different
parts in the stack.

00:07:17.541 --> 00:07:20.390
I think it was
a very a fertile place

00:07:20.391 --> 00:07:21.800
to have group meetings
because you'd

00:07:21.801 --> 00:07:23.630
have to explain things
carefully across

00:07:23.631 --> 00:07:26.300
those lines which makes
you a better communicator,

00:07:26.301 --> 00:07:27.500
but you also come up with

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:29.930
interesting research ideas that

00:07:29.931 --> 00:07:33.220
lie in the interstices between
different topic areas.

00:07:33.221 --> 00:07:35.090
&gt;&gt; I think it's
the first time I've heard

00:07:35.091 --> 00:07:38.285
diversity being
different parts of the stack.

00:07:38.286 --> 00:07:40.815
It's pretty fun Margaret.

00:07:40.816 --> 00:07:44.270
Well, Jim, what about
you? Tell us your story.

00:07:44.271 --> 00:07:46.820
&gt;&gt; Okay. Well, before
I say a story,

00:07:46.821 --> 00:07:49.210
I have to set the stage
for the story, right?

00:07:49.211 --> 00:07:49.550
&gt;&gt; Okay. Of course.

00:07:49.551 --> 00:07:50.600
&gt;&gt; I think something
that we're seeing in

00:07:50.601 --> 00:07:52.790
computing now is a sort of

00:07:52.791 --> 00:07:54.740
a move from computing being tools

00:07:54.741 --> 00:07:56.680
to computing being
partners with people.

00:07:56.681 --> 00:07:58.340
At the National
Science Foundation

00:07:58.341 --> 00:07:59.780
where I'm working right now.

00:07:59.781 --> 00:08:02.480
We talk about the human
technology frontier and we talk

00:08:02.481 --> 00:08:05.330
about technology that
really means computing.

00:08:05.331 --> 00:08:07.350
Maybe we shouldn't say that to

00:08:07.351 --> 00:08:09.350
everybody across
the National Science Foundation.

00:08:09.351 --> 00:08:11.395
But that's science, that's
what we think about,

00:08:11.396 --> 00:08:14.500
and when we think about
the human technology frontier,

00:08:14.501 --> 00:08:15.660
it's all of humanity.

00:08:15.661 --> 00:08:17.495
So in a sense, I take it

00:08:17.496 --> 00:08:19.520
to be really axiomatic
that if we're

00:08:19.521 --> 00:08:22.505
building tools that
we're going to

00:08:22.506 --> 00:08:24.050
partner with as humans

00:08:24.051 --> 00:08:25.930
that it's going to be
for all of humanity.

00:08:25.931 --> 00:08:29.450
So it's axiomatic that
we want to embrace and

00:08:29.451 --> 00:08:32.210
engage across
all different aspects

00:08:32.211 --> 00:08:33.995
of diversity whether
it be cultural,

00:08:33.996 --> 00:08:37.250
socio-economic, gender,
however you measure it.

00:08:37.251 --> 00:08:40.520
So, my story, my favorite
project I worked on,

00:08:40.521 --> 00:08:43.650
actually it was all the way
up and down the stack also.

00:08:43.651 --> 00:08:45.945
At the University
of Massachusetts,

00:08:45.946 --> 00:08:47.690
we had a project on

00:08:47.691 --> 00:08:50.710
collaborative adaptive
sensing of the atmosphere.

00:08:50.711 --> 00:08:53.000
It was about the
system concept versus

00:08:53.001 --> 00:08:56.090
a dense network of low-powered
radars that would sense

00:08:56.091 --> 00:08:59.720
hazardous weather and predict
and do early warning and

00:08:59.721 --> 00:09:03.740
detection of hazardous weather
and learning how to folks.

00:09:03.741 --> 00:09:06.095
To me that's
sort of a quintessential

00:09:06.096 --> 00:09:08.555
socio-technical smack at the dab,

00:09:08.556 --> 00:09:11.545
smack dab at the human
technology frontier,

00:09:11.546 --> 00:09:12.470
and it was diverse.

00:09:12.471 --> 00:09:14.350
So yes, I went all the way
up and down the stack,

00:09:14.351 --> 00:09:16.220
all the way from
designing wave forms to

00:09:16.221 --> 00:09:19.200
deciding when to issue
warnings to people.

00:09:19.201 --> 00:09:20.925
It was also diverse

00:09:20.926 --> 00:09:25.760
in the backgrounds of
people who were there.

00:09:25.761 --> 00:09:28.160
We had meteorologists, we
had computer scientists,

00:09:28.161 --> 00:09:29.290
that was me in the terms

00:09:29.291 --> 00:09:31.260
of the leadership,
meteorologists,

00:09:31.261 --> 00:09:35.395
computer scientists, atmospheric
scientists, sociologists,

00:09:35.396 --> 00:09:37.910
electrical engineers, all
working side by side.

00:09:37.911 --> 00:09:40.130
We had, let's see,

00:09:40.131 --> 00:09:43.045
an Asian- American, that was
me and the Indian-American,

00:09:43.046 --> 00:09:46.405
we had a Latino and African-American
and two white males

00:09:46.406 --> 00:09:49.380
in our leadership team.

00:09:49.600 --> 00:09:54.140
So, I think we just had
incredible diversity in that,

00:09:54.141 --> 00:09:57.560
and I think of all of the
projects that I've worked on,

00:09:57.561 --> 00:09:59.570
to me that was the
most rewarding because

00:09:59.571 --> 00:10:01.750
of all of the diverse viewpoints,

00:10:01.751 --> 00:10:03.790
we had to bring to
solve that and system.

00:10:03.791 --> 00:10:06.255
All the way from
designing radar forms,

00:10:06.256 --> 00:10:08.130
all the way up to
doing notifications.

00:10:08.131 --> 00:10:10.280
&gt;&gt; So interdisciplinary
also seems to be

00:10:10.281 --> 00:10:12.690
coming out in
this conversation as well.

00:10:12.691 --> 00:10:14.695
So, Abolade, tell us your story.

00:10:14.696 --> 00:10:17.590
&gt;&gt; I also have
an interdisciplinary story.

00:10:17.591 --> 00:10:20.730
So, we see, I think
the things that you shared,

00:10:20.731 --> 00:10:22.570
Gem and Margaret,
really resonated.

00:10:22.571 --> 00:10:26.605
The project that I'm thinking
about is project Rome,

00:10:26.606 --> 00:10:31.925
which was an effort that we
started to really rethink how

00:10:31.926 --> 00:10:34.180
you interact with your computer

00:10:34.181 --> 00:10:37.120
in a world where you
don't just have one,

00:10:37.121 --> 00:10:38.510
where you have several.

00:10:38.511 --> 00:10:40.880
You're doing things on
your mobile device,

00:10:40.881 --> 00:10:43.030
you are doing things
maybe on a laptop,

00:10:43.031 --> 00:10:45.775
maybe you have a workstation
where you do other things.

00:10:45.776 --> 00:10:48.440
As we got started
on this project,

00:10:48.441 --> 00:10:50.590
one of the first things
that we did is we assembled

00:10:50.591 --> 00:10:53.800
a team; folks from marketing,

00:10:53.801 --> 00:10:56.185
folks from user
experience design,

00:10:56.186 --> 00:10:58.110
folks like me who
are plumbers who

00:10:58.111 --> 00:11:01.080
understand the operating system
and communications,

00:11:01.081 --> 00:11:04.810
and we started by trying
to tell the story.

00:11:04.811 --> 00:11:07.950
What we found was that
by trying to tell

00:11:07.951 --> 00:11:11.590
this story first,
that multidisciplinary approach,

00:11:11.591 --> 00:11:14.810
we picked the problems
that we wanted to

00:11:14.811 --> 00:11:18.725
solve in a much better way
and we were able to focus.

00:11:18.726 --> 00:11:20.315
Some of you may have seen we

00:11:20.316 --> 00:11:22.930
launched a feature recently
called "Your Phone",

00:11:22.931 --> 00:11:24.420
that lets you link your phone and

00:11:24.421 --> 00:11:26.290
PC and use them seamlessly,

00:11:26.291 --> 00:11:28.300
get messages on your PC,

00:11:28.301 --> 00:11:30.905
see photos on your PC
from your phone.

00:11:30.906 --> 00:11:32.200
That in a lot of ways is the

00:11:32.201 --> 00:11:33.710
culmination of a journey that

00:11:33.711 --> 00:11:36.750
started from taking
a slightly different approach.

00:11:36.751 --> 00:11:39.135
Traditionally, in
the operating system,

00:11:39.136 --> 00:11:41.074
we would start with
the technology,

00:11:41.075 --> 00:11:44.030
and then we would go
looking for the problem.

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:49.325
In this case, I think
it was eye-opening,

00:11:49.326 --> 00:11:52.580
how much of a difference it
made in the final result,

00:11:52.581 --> 00:11:55.400
just assembling
a different set of

00:11:55.401 --> 00:11:58.805
perspectives at an early phase,

00:11:58.806 --> 00:12:00.940
in going after a problem.

00:12:00.941 --> 00:12:04.160
&gt;&gt; Excuse me. So,
it's so interesting,

00:12:04.161 --> 00:12:06.095
I love operating systems too.

00:12:06.096 --> 00:12:07.890
I'd rather just do
operating systems,

00:12:07.891 --> 00:12:11.380
let the problems find
me, that's more fun.

00:12:11.381 --> 00:12:15.250
Just a joke. Anyway, so

00:12:15.251 --> 00:12:16.910
when I was at Bell Labs
in the early 90's,

00:12:16.911 --> 00:12:17.750
I did a project,

00:12:17.751 --> 00:12:20.830
it was a Multimedia
Communication Server Enterprise.

00:12:20.831 --> 00:12:26.565
It was a mash-up between Skype
and Google Docs in 1993,

00:12:26.566 --> 00:12:32.405
and the team was hugely
diverse: racial diversity,

00:12:32.406 --> 00:12:35.830
gender diversity, all kinds
of ethnicities on this team.

00:12:35.831 --> 00:12:39.030
It was really that project
that got me thinking about,

00:12:39.031 --> 00:12:40.185
I was a bit of a slow learner,

00:12:40.186 --> 00:12:41.230
you're like "Wait a minute.

00:12:41.231 --> 00:12:42.555
Wow. This is awesome,

00:12:42.556 --> 00:12:44.210
all these different
kinds of people."

00:12:44.211 --> 00:12:46.015
All of us had great diversity,

00:12:46.016 --> 00:12:47.690
and a lot of that came because

00:12:47.691 --> 00:12:50.050
we hired really smart thinkers,

00:12:50.051 --> 00:12:51.700
mathematicians, scientists,

00:12:51.701 --> 00:12:54.340
and they learned how to
create hardware and software.

00:12:54.341 --> 00:12:56.200
Yes, so, it was very diverse.

00:12:56.201 --> 00:12:57.460
It was a really
interesting project.

00:12:57.461 --> 00:13:00.160
Though unfortunately,
not all teams are this way,

00:13:00.161 --> 00:13:02.380
we know from our stats,

00:13:02.381 --> 00:13:04.510
and I'm going to give you
a few stats just to put

00:13:04.511 --> 00:13:07.630
a little foundational
grounding under this.

00:13:07.631 --> 00:13:10.455
I'm going to give you some
stats that are about gender,

00:13:10.456 --> 00:13:12.475
but I also want to
reinforce again,

00:13:12.476 --> 00:13:15.830
we need stats on all
groups participation.

00:13:15.831 --> 00:13:17.380
There are number of historically

00:13:17.381 --> 00:13:19.135
marginalized groups in computing,

00:13:19.136 --> 00:13:21.440
in this particular case
and these case studies,

00:13:21.441 --> 00:13:23.160
and what they really
tell you, the

00:13:23.161 --> 00:13:26.280
process we really
only look at gender.

00:13:26.281 --> 00:13:29.280
But, their innovation metrics
are different than

00:13:29.281 --> 00:13:31.340
participation metrics
which makes them

00:13:31.341 --> 00:13:33.990
different than the ones
we normally look at.

00:13:33.991 --> 00:13:35.470
So, this is really around

00:13:35.471 --> 00:13:38.250
more meaningful
participation in computing.

00:13:38.251 --> 00:13:40.430
So, NC runs a patenting study,

00:13:40.431 --> 00:13:42.705
we update it every
couple of years.

00:13:42.706 --> 00:13:44.870
We crawl the US Patent database

00:13:44.871 --> 00:13:46.220
with gender matching software,

00:13:46.221 --> 00:13:48.055
and we assign a gender to

00:13:48.056 --> 00:13:51.705
every inventor on every IT patent

00:13:51.706 --> 00:13:53.500
and then we look at the results.

00:13:53.501 --> 00:13:56.310
IT is a government's
definition of IT,

00:13:56.311 --> 00:13:57.585
so it's all things computing.

00:13:57.586 --> 00:14:00.955
It's everything in
the computing community,

00:14:00.956 --> 00:14:03.355
and end result over
the last 30 years:

00:14:03.356 --> 00:14:08.095
88 percent of all patents have
male only invention teams,

00:14:08.096 --> 00:14:10.150
well, that's one or more men,

00:14:10.151 --> 00:14:12.630
two percent have
female only invention teams,

00:14:12.631 --> 00:14:14.420
and the rest are mixed.

00:14:14.421 --> 00:14:19.175
So, of course, patenting is
only one innovation metric.

00:14:19.176 --> 00:14:22.490
We did the same thing on
the ACM paper database,

00:14:22.491 --> 00:14:23.920
for papers accepted to

00:14:23.921 --> 00:14:27.460
ACM conferences and we did
the same type of thing,

00:14:27.461 --> 00:14:29.600
that N of over 600,000,

00:14:29.601 --> 00:14:31.055
it was a pretty big N,

00:14:31.056 --> 00:14:33.730
and about 78 percent of

00:14:33.731 --> 00:14:37.000
all authors on
accepted papers to ACM,

00:14:37.001 --> 00:14:39.020
about 78 percent are

00:14:39.021 --> 00:14:43.230
male and the rest are
female, 22 percent.

00:14:43.231 --> 00:14:46.545
Now, that number's not,
you'd like it to be higher,

00:14:46.546 --> 00:14:47.760
let's just face it.

00:14:47.761 --> 00:14:49.230
But it's not so bad when you

00:14:49.231 --> 00:14:50.990
consider women's participation in

00:14:50.991 --> 00:14:54.430
Computing Faculty in
major research institutions.

00:14:54.431 --> 00:14:57.245
It tracks about what
you would expect,

00:14:57.246 --> 00:14:59.600
but the real thing on
this particular study was

00:14:59.601 --> 00:15:03.395
the sub-segregation of papers
between women and men.

00:15:03.396 --> 00:15:05.240
Women were mostly in

00:15:05.241 --> 00:15:08.020
documentation human computer
interface not systems,

00:15:08.021 --> 00:15:10.070
not networking, not algorithms.

00:15:10.071 --> 00:15:11.950
So, huge sub-segregation.

00:15:11.951 --> 00:15:13.870
Real quickly, just to let you

00:15:13.871 --> 00:15:16.345
know, overall
participation numbers,

00:15:16.346 --> 00:15:19.850
women make up about 13 percent
of all hardware developers

00:15:19.851 --> 00:15:23.375
and about 18 percent of
all software developers.

00:15:23.376 --> 00:15:25.280
So, it's pretty safe to

00:15:25.281 --> 00:15:27.700
say with numbers like
these and others,

00:15:27.701 --> 00:15:29.565
that we're missing out on

00:15:29.566 --> 00:15:32.930
a large set of
creative ideas from

00:15:32.931 --> 00:15:35.265
over half the population
when it comes to

00:15:35.266 --> 00:15:38.845
innovation and research in
the areas we care about.

00:15:38.846 --> 00:15:41.185
So, that's like
a huge untapped potential

00:15:41.186 --> 00:15:43.770
that if we could just
figure out what to do here,

00:15:43.771 --> 00:15:45.520
there's an innovation gap,

00:15:45.521 --> 00:15:48.910
and a huge upside
potential if we solve it.

00:15:48.911 --> 00:15:52.835
So, my second question is
going to be, if I get to it.

00:15:52.836 --> 00:16:00.450
Here we go, as panelists and
as innovators yourselves,

00:16:00.451 --> 00:16:02.950
why do you think it's

00:16:02.951 --> 00:16:04.600
critical to close
this innovation gap?

00:16:04.601 --> 00:16:05.660
You've hinted at some of that in

00:16:05.661 --> 00:16:07.765
the answers to
your first questions,

00:16:07.766 --> 00:16:09.890
why do you think it's
critical to close

00:16:09.891 --> 00:16:13.210
this and why do you
think we have this?

00:16:13.211 --> 00:16:14.445
Why do you think it's happening?

00:16:14.446 --> 00:16:17.715
I'm going first Abolande

00:16:17.716 --> 00:16:18.195
&gt;&gt; Alright.

00:16:18.196 --> 00:16:18.905
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:16:18.906 --> 00:16:22.495
&gt;&gt; The word that comes to
mind to me when I think about

00:16:22.496 --> 00:16:27.420
the importance of diversity
in innovation, is robustness.

00:16:27.421 --> 00:16:31.035
If you think about an idea
as a living thing,

00:16:31.036 --> 00:16:35.355
it's survivability
depends heavily

00:16:35.356 --> 00:16:38.370
on its composition at birth.

00:16:38.371 --> 00:16:41.210
Ideas that come out
of diverse teams,

00:16:41.211 --> 00:16:42.730
ideas that reflect at

00:16:42.731 --> 00:16:45.370
birth a set of
diverse perspectives,

00:16:45.371 --> 00:16:48.220
not just a coder's

00:16:48.221 --> 00:16:50.730
perspective on
what algorithm he can write,

00:16:50.731 --> 00:16:53.500
but say a marketing
person's perspective on

00:16:53.501 --> 00:16:56.925
what things matter to end users,

00:16:56.926 --> 00:17:00.885
human-computer interaction
specialists' perspective

00:17:00.886 --> 00:17:03.425
on what is usable,

00:17:03.426 --> 00:17:06.800
a man's perspective, a
woman's perspective,

00:17:06.801 --> 00:17:10.620
an African's perspective,
various backgrounds.

00:17:10.621 --> 00:17:15.590
When all of those are present
at the birth of an idea,

00:17:15.591 --> 00:17:18.355
it's the likelihood
that it will not

00:17:18.356 --> 00:17:22.460
die on the way to
adulthood is much higher.

00:17:22.461 --> 00:17:25.360
I think that metaphor
is helpful because,

00:17:25.361 --> 00:17:28.520
really I think what we care
about is not just innovation,

00:17:28.521 --> 00:17:31.495
but innovation that has impact.

00:17:31.496 --> 00:17:34.045
Innovation that has impact

00:17:34.046 --> 00:17:36.950
has the characteristic,
to me at least,

00:17:36.951 --> 00:17:39.930
that there are things that
have been thought through and

00:17:39.931 --> 00:17:42.470
incorporated at
every stage along the way,

00:17:42.471 --> 00:17:44.440
that really can
only get in there,

00:17:44.441 --> 00:17:46.869
that can only really be woven in,

00:17:46.870 --> 00:17:48.330
if you have a set of

00:17:48.331 --> 00:17:50.940
diverse perspectives that
reflects the world in which

00:17:50.941 --> 00:17:53.595
that idea is going to actually

00:17:53.596 --> 00:17:56.395
exist. There was
another question.

00:17:56.396 --> 00:17:58.150
&gt;&gt; I know. It's
a double-barreled question

00:17:58.151 --> 00:17:59.225
which is really unfair.

00:17:59.226 --> 00:18:02.920
So, the second part
of the question was,

00:18:02.921 --> 00:18:04.950
why do you think
we have this gap?

00:18:04.951 --> 00:18:06.620
&gt;&gt; I think it's familiarity.

00:18:06.621 --> 00:18:08.940
I think that the easiest thing to

00:18:08.941 --> 00:18:11.515
do is what you tend
to see the most of.

00:18:11.516 --> 00:18:13.230
I think it takes real effort

00:18:13.231 --> 00:18:16.370
to apply a
multidisciplinary approach.

00:18:16.371 --> 00:18:19.180
I think it takes
real effort to number one,

00:18:19.181 --> 00:18:20.910
identify a lack of

00:18:20.911 --> 00:18:23.620
diversity and then
number two, correct it.

00:18:23.621 --> 00:18:25.660
You have to be intentional
about it and I

00:18:25.661 --> 00:18:28.175
think that without
the right mindset

00:18:28.176 --> 00:18:30.595
and without the right
framework around

00:18:30.596 --> 00:18:34.575
optimizing for diversity
in our approach,

00:18:34.576 --> 00:18:38.460
I think the default behaviors
tend to dominate.

00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:41.360
&gt;&gt; So, I might answer

00:18:41.361 --> 00:18:44.040
the second question first
by how did we get here?

00:18:44.041 --> 00:18:45.595
I think it ties back to this

00:18:45.596 --> 00:18:48.530
this notion of the human
technology frontier,

00:18:48.531 --> 00:18:51.080
and a shift from focusing

00:18:51.081 --> 00:18:53.930
more on computing, and databases,

00:18:53.931 --> 00:18:55.855
and operating system
networks as artifacts,

00:18:55.856 --> 00:18:57.940
and more now towards computing,

00:18:57.941 --> 00:19:00.820
partnering with humans, and
more on the application side.

00:19:00.821 --> 00:19:04.000
I think, we were able
to maybe slide by

00:19:04.001 --> 00:19:06.210
without as much diversity

00:19:06.211 --> 00:19:08.490
before because it was more
looking at artifacts.

00:19:08.491 --> 00:19:10.855
Now, if we're building

00:19:10.856 --> 00:19:13.540
technologies that are going
to partner with humans,

00:19:13.541 --> 00:19:15.460
there's just absolutely
no way we can

00:19:15.461 --> 00:19:17.625
get by without
that firstly perspectives.

00:19:17.626 --> 00:19:19.195
&gt;&gt; I think that's
a really good point.

00:19:19.196 --> 00:19:20.540
Margaret, what what do you think?

00:19:20.541 --> 00:19:22.020
&gt;&gt; I wanted to zoom in on

00:19:22.021 --> 00:19:24.230
a particular part of
the timeline which is

00:19:24.231 --> 00:19:28.775
the time from late in a PhD
onwards into one's career.

00:19:28.776 --> 00:19:30.510
I want to zoom in
in particular on

00:19:30.511 --> 00:19:33.650
the systems research
community writ large.

00:19:33.651 --> 00:19:37.045
I want to say that it is really

00:19:37.046 --> 00:19:39.650
easy to think about

00:19:39.651 --> 00:19:41.960
the numbers that we have
in our undergrad programs,

00:19:41.961 --> 00:19:44.060
which thankfully, are
coming back up again.

00:19:44.061 --> 00:19:46.985
Actually, first thing
is concrete example.

00:19:46.986 --> 00:19:49.550
I represent the peak year in

00:19:49.551 --> 00:19:52.055
terms of percentage
production of women,

00:19:52.056 --> 00:19:54.900
CS bachelor's degrees in America.

00:19:54.901 --> 00:19:58.725
I'm 53 years-old,
the peak was 1986.

00:19:58.726 --> 00:20:00.980
We came back down for many,

00:20:00.981 --> 00:20:03.245
many years, and now
we're coming back up.

00:20:03.246 --> 00:20:04.680
So, first of all,

00:20:04.681 --> 00:20:07.120
there's this notion that
the pipeline will save us,

00:20:07.121 --> 00:20:09.340
but the pipeline was draining.

00:20:09.341 --> 00:20:11.685
Second of all, the other
part of it is that

00:20:11.686 --> 00:20:14.380
PhD production has
hung in there at

00:20:14.381 --> 00:20:17.180
around 15 to 20% women for

00:20:17.181 --> 00:20:20.345
many years because we
refill internationally,

00:20:20.346 --> 00:20:25.110
even though US numbers
undergrad have fluctuated.

00:20:25.111 --> 00:20:28.070
So, you look in a later part
of the career and you say,

00:20:28.071 --> 00:20:30.560
"We could've been
sort of sustaining

00:20:30.561 --> 00:20:32.900
about 20% participation in

00:20:32.901 --> 00:20:34.840
our systems research community."

00:20:34.841 --> 00:20:39.080
But if you look at
the ACM sig membership numbers,

00:20:39.081 --> 00:20:43.625
of the sigs that we
represent in this room,

00:20:43.626 --> 00:20:47.380
SigOps, SigPlan and SigCom

00:20:47.381 --> 00:20:50.485
have less than five
percent women membership.

00:20:50.486 --> 00:20:55.690
SigArch comes in, that's my sig
at around eight percent,

00:20:55.691 --> 00:21:00.695
and kudos to SigMobile which
tops out just above 10%.

00:21:00.696 --> 00:21:02.820
So, something's
happening in how we're

00:21:02.821 --> 00:21:05.825
cultivating the second
part of a person's career.

00:21:05.826 --> 00:21:09.030
Like we get them to the point
of getting a PhD but do we

00:21:09.031 --> 00:21:13.320
cultivate what sustains
them longer term than that,

00:21:13.321 --> 00:21:15.130
that's not as clear to me that

00:21:15.131 --> 00:21:16.620
we are doing as
much in the way of

00:21:16.621 --> 00:21:19.910
long-term mentoring, and
long-term appreciation.

00:21:19.911 --> 00:21:22.470
I think the first step is,

00:21:22.471 --> 00:21:25.580
as sustenance researchers we
measure what we care about.

00:21:25.581 --> 00:21:26.920
So, we should be measuring more

00:21:26.921 --> 00:21:28.250
of this data in ongoing way.

00:21:28.251 --> 00:21:30.055
We should get it for
under-represented groups,

00:21:30.056 --> 00:21:32.050
so that we can see
at a conference,

00:21:32.051 --> 00:21:34.160
how many of the first authors

00:21:34.161 --> 00:21:36.135
presenting the paper were women?

00:21:36.136 --> 00:21:37.460
Are under-represented minorities?

00:21:37.461 --> 00:21:39.050
It's often very small,

00:21:39.051 --> 00:21:41.095
and it's very hard to know.

00:21:41.096 --> 00:21:44.960
So, I think better data
tracking used to

00:21:44.961 --> 00:21:46.690
motivate that mentorship and

00:21:46.691 --> 00:21:49.650
sustained career development
would be great.

00:21:49.651 --> 00:21:51.835
&gt;&gt; You're touching
someone culture.

00:21:51.836 --> 00:21:54.510
So, we again, to really not

00:21:54.511 --> 00:21:57.865
focus on the educational
pipeline but what happens after.

00:21:57.866 --> 00:22:02.250
I can say a bit about the
workforce, corporate workforce.

00:22:02.251 --> 00:22:04.450
One of the biggest
things that we hear

00:22:04.451 --> 00:22:07.465
there on hospitable culture,

00:22:07.466 --> 00:22:10.890
when you start to
really dig into that,

00:22:10.891 --> 00:22:13.445
you find out that, for example,

00:22:13.446 --> 00:22:16.735
women tend to be more in
the support roles that support

00:22:16.736 --> 00:22:20.275
the creative endeavors
of innovators.

00:22:20.276 --> 00:22:21.030
So they would be in

00:22:21.031 --> 00:22:23.305
project management
system verification,

00:22:23.306 --> 00:22:24.820
customer requirements,

00:22:24.821 --> 00:22:26.840
very important things by
the way, don't get me wrong.

00:22:26.841 --> 00:22:28.440
Super important, but they're

00:22:28.441 --> 00:22:29.940
not the things that would lead

00:22:29.941 --> 00:22:34.080
generally to a promotion
up the technical ladder.

00:22:34.081 --> 00:22:38.330
So, what happens that to
be a CTO, or an architect,

00:22:38.331 --> 00:22:39.930
or distinguished engineer,

00:22:39.931 --> 00:22:42.495
they just don't lead
there, generally speaking.

00:22:42.496 --> 00:22:45.765
So, by mid-career they get
frustrated and they leave.

00:22:45.766 --> 00:22:48.260
So, they're not even
at the stage of

00:22:48.261 --> 00:22:50.400
their career yet where
they could be innovators.

00:22:50.401 --> 00:22:52.320
They don't have
the task assignments.

00:22:52.321 --> 00:22:55.590
When you drill down into
that, while the supervisors,

00:22:55.591 --> 00:22:58.410
technical supervisors really
have no idea how they assign

00:22:58.411 --> 00:23:01.380
roles, tasks, assignments etc.

00:23:01.381 --> 00:23:02.700
So, those are the kinds of

00:23:02.701 --> 00:23:04.040
things we have to
really start being

00:23:04.041 --> 00:23:07.410
curious about and
all the people are analytical.

00:23:07.411 --> 00:23:09.495
You all have a great
spirit of inquiry.

00:23:09.496 --> 00:23:11.730
If we start really getting
a data, and digging into it,

00:23:11.731 --> 00:23:13.195
and being curious about it,

00:23:13.196 --> 00:23:14.340
as Margaret is saying,

00:23:14.341 --> 00:23:16.010
and look for some of the ways we

00:23:16.011 --> 00:23:17.930
can take bias out of
some of these systems,

00:23:17.931 --> 00:23:20.470
I think the gap will will close.

00:23:20.471 --> 00:23:23.260
But we have to really be curious
and analytical about it.

00:23:23.261 --> 00:23:24.650
Just in the same
when you are around

00:23:24.651 --> 00:23:28.150
cloud computing or anything else.

00:23:28.151 --> 00:23:30.220
So, that's what we
see in the workforce,

00:23:30.221 --> 00:23:33.210
and I suspect it's somewhat
true in academia as well.

00:23:33.211 --> 00:23:35.740
Will anybody want to comment
on what the culture is like

00:23:35.741 --> 00:23:38.950
for young researchers
and old researchers,

00:23:38.951 --> 00:23:42.455
or new PhDs in academia?

00:23:42.456 --> 00:23:46.175
&gt;&gt; So, I'm a lifelong
unapologetic academic.

00:23:46.176 --> 00:23:47.470
I love it.

00:23:47.471 --> 00:23:50.030
I think it's the best job
in the world.

00:23:50.031 --> 00:23:53.480
Frankly, I think we do
an okay job in many ways

00:23:53.481 --> 00:23:57.105
of cultivating
our young faculty members.

00:23:57.106 --> 00:24:00.560
I sometimes worry that

00:24:00.561 --> 00:24:03.305
we don't explicitly say
how much fun we're having.

00:24:03.306 --> 00:24:05.700
I try to explicitly say
how much fun I'm having.

00:24:05.701 --> 00:24:07.430
Because I think people
need to see that.

00:24:07.431 --> 00:24:10.320
You want to engage in
a path that: A) shows

00:24:10.321 --> 00:24:14.620
impact and B) looks enjoyable.

00:24:14.621 --> 00:24:19.170
So, I try to model that
more explicitly myself.

00:24:19.171 --> 00:24:20.680
I think in academia,

00:24:20.681 --> 00:24:22.130
there are flexibilities to

00:24:22.131 --> 00:24:23.980
the way you operate
groups and the way

00:24:23.981 --> 00:24:27.875
so you manage your time that
are incredibly compelling.

00:24:27.876 --> 00:24:31.755
Getting the word out about
that is super important.

00:24:31.756 --> 00:24:34.805
&gt;&gt; So, tell us what NSF
doing in this space?

00:24:34.806 --> 00:24:37.060
You're well known for
broadening participation.

00:24:37.061 --> 00:24:38.290
&gt;&gt; Let me answer
the NSF question.

00:24:38.291 --> 00:24:39.710
But I will say that
like Margaret,

00:24:39.711 --> 00:24:42.330
I'm a lifelong academic
and absolutely love it.

00:24:42.331 --> 00:24:44.840
Many of the challenges
that I think we face with

00:24:44.841 --> 00:24:47.935
diversity in computing
are faced across,

00:24:47.936 --> 00:24:50.185
really across the university.

00:24:50.186 --> 00:24:52.230
My sense is that universities

00:24:52.231 --> 00:24:54.460
are becoming more
attuned to that,

00:24:54.461 --> 00:24:58.385
and more flexible in hiring
and tenure processes.

00:24:58.386 --> 00:25:01.060
So, I see some light at
the end of the tunnel there.

00:25:01.061 --> 00:25:02.740
Let me put on my NSF hat and say

00:25:02.741 --> 00:25:04.520
a couple words about NSF.

00:25:04.521 --> 00:25:06.700
So, there's a long history.

00:25:06.701 --> 00:25:09.460
Well,10 years I suppose
it's not that long,

00:25:09.461 --> 00:25:12.560
but certainly a recent
and really storied

00:25:12.561 --> 00:25:14.075
history of activities here

00:25:14.076 --> 00:25:16.000
in broadening participation
in computing there,

00:25:16.001 --> 00:25:20.275
the BPC alliances like
NC Whit, like CRAW,

00:25:20.276 --> 00:25:23.560
access computing cache
and others,

00:25:23.561 --> 00:25:25.480
and these have been
ongoing activities for 10 years

00:25:25.481 --> 00:25:28.080
that I just think I've
been absolutely fabulous.

00:25:28.081 --> 00:25:29.220
There are mentoring program,

00:25:29.221 --> 00:25:32.665
so Margaret mentioned
the importance of mentoring.

00:25:32.666 --> 00:25:35.770
At the undergraduate level,
career mentoring,

00:25:35.771 --> 00:25:37.360
grad women's cohorts,

00:25:37.361 --> 00:25:40.385
so projects and
activities like that.

00:25:40.386 --> 00:25:42.340
We're not talking much
about the pipeline

00:25:42.341 --> 00:25:43.630
but I can't help

00:25:43.631 --> 00:25:46.650
but mention "Computer
Science for All,"

00:25:46.651 --> 00:25:48.745
and we'd like to
really say "For all"

00:25:48.746 --> 00:25:50.800
to emphasize the equity and

00:25:50.801 --> 00:25:53.030
the access to

00:25:53.031 --> 00:25:56.435
rigorous and really compelling
computer science education

00:25:56.436 --> 00:25:57.670
in K 212,

00:25:57.671 --> 00:25:59.350
and that's an activity that

00:25:59.351 --> 00:26:00.930
we're really, really proud of.

00:26:00.931 --> 00:26:03.180
One of the things that's
happened there in addition to

00:26:03.181 --> 00:26:05.720
the ECS computing that
folks may have heard about,

00:26:05.721 --> 00:26:07.170
the computer science principles,

00:26:07.171 --> 00:26:08.905
a second AP exam,

00:26:08.906 --> 00:26:11.315
this offered
the first time in 2017.

00:26:11.316 --> 00:26:13.685
Fifty thousand students took it,

00:26:13.686 --> 00:26:16.695
between what, 2017 and 2016,

00:26:16.696 --> 00:26:20.760
the number of students
taking the AP exam,

00:26:20.761 --> 00:26:21.890
either the AP exams and

00:26:21.891 --> 00:26:23.440
computer science
more than double,

00:26:23.441 --> 00:26:24.940
the number of women
more than doubled,

00:26:24.941 --> 00:26:27.120
the number of Hispanic students
more than doubled,

00:26:27.121 --> 00:26:28.560
the number of
African-American students.

00:26:28.561 --> 00:26:31.360
So, I think that speaks
really well for the pipeline.

00:26:31.361 --> 00:26:33.965
I will tell you since many
of you are academics here,

00:26:33.966 --> 00:26:35.750
the challenge is going to be when

00:26:35.751 --> 00:26:38.595
those students show up on
our academic doorsteps.

00:26:38.596 --> 00:26:41.735
Are we going to have
programs that welcome them,

00:26:41.736 --> 00:26:44.090
and the kinds of computing
that that they want to do.

00:26:44.091 --> 00:26:46.650
I see that as a challenge.

00:26:46.651 --> 00:26:51.080
I think as wonderful
as those are,

00:26:52.200 --> 00:26:55.035
sometimes it's too easy
to sit here and say,

00:26:55.036 --> 00:26:57.695
"Oh, look at all those great
things that are going on.

00:26:57.696 --> 00:26:59.300
The Lucy Sanders
of the world will

00:26:59.301 --> 00:27:02.200
take care of BPC for us. "

00:27:02.201 --> 00:27:04.125
They do wonderful things.

00:27:04.126 --> 00:27:06.030
But it's a challenge
for all of us I

00:27:06.031 --> 00:27:09.780
think in looking out here,

00:27:09.781 --> 00:27:11.990
all of us here have

00:27:11.991 --> 00:27:17.285
a responsibility and
a need to address that.

00:27:17.286 --> 00:27:19.100
So, a new program actually that

00:27:19.101 --> 00:27:22.155
we're piloting at
the National Science Foundation,

00:27:22.156 --> 00:27:23.880
I know many people
here don't read

00:27:23.881 --> 00:27:25.540
the dear colleague letters
that I write,

00:27:25.541 --> 00:27:27.980
but you should read
dear colleague letter

00:27:27.981 --> 00:27:33.020
17110 on engaging in

00:27:33.021 --> 00:27:36.060
meaningful broadening
participation activities.

00:27:36.061 --> 00:27:38.275
What we're trying to do
here is to make this,

00:27:38.276 --> 00:27:40.640
what we NSF are
trying to do here,

00:27:40.641 --> 00:27:42.890
we've piloted this in
the very large grants,

00:27:42.891 --> 00:27:44.740
we're going to pilot it
next year in some of

00:27:44.741 --> 00:27:48.105
the select mediums,
and enlarge grants.

00:27:48.106 --> 00:27:51.910
The idea here is that
everybody who's funded under

00:27:51.911 --> 00:27:53.980
an NSF grant should take part in

00:27:53.981 --> 00:27:57.145
some meaningful BPC activity.

00:27:57.146 --> 00:27:58.980
It doesn't mean you have
to go out and start

00:27:58.981 --> 00:28:01.590
the next MC whit or CRAW.

00:28:01.591 --> 00:28:04.140
But there's something
that every one of us can

00:28:04.141 --> 00:28:07.520
do to help broaden participation.

00:28:07.521 --> 00:28:08.860
It can be in many
of the ones that

00:28:08.861 --> 00:28:10.530
Margaret just
mentioned, for example.

00:28:10.531 --> 00:28:13.655
So, we're really very
excited about that.

00:28:13.656 --> 00:28:16.450
So, if I could leave
you with one thought,

00:28:16.451 --> 00:28:19.485
had been a teacher
for 32 years I guess,

00:28:19.486 --> 00:28:21.395
if you walk away with one thing,

00:28:21.396 --> 00:28:22.885
here's one thing to walk away.

00:28:22.886 --> 00:28:24.190
Read dear colleague letter

00:28:24.191 --> 00:28:27.985
17110 or send me an email
and I'll send it to you.

00:28:27.986 --> 00:28:30.820
&gt;&gt; That's great. What's
the number again?

00:28:30.821 --> 00:28:32.590
&gt;&gt; 17110.

00:28:32.591 --> 00:28:33.760
&gt;&gt; Okay. [OVERLAPPING]

00:28:33.761 --> 00:28:35.130
&gt;&gt; If we have this first,

00:28:35.131 --> 00:28:37.435
I'll have the number
there on the slide.

00:28:37.436 --> 00:28:39.380
&gt;&gt; We should put it on
announcement stab or

00:28:39.381 --> 00:28:43.850
the Microsoft research
faculty research,

00:28:43.851 --> 00:28:45.070
say that would be great.

00:28:45.071 --> 00:28:46.540
I want to just throw in

00:28:46.541 --> 00:28:48.270
a one question before I

00:28:48.271 --> 00:28:49.940
ask the final question
because I don't want

00:28:49.941 --> 00:28:52.900
to leave the panel
without maybe ask you for

00:28:52.901 --> 00:28:54.875
a few observations on

00:28:54.876 --> 00:28:57.790
what the researchers will
call intersectionality.

00:28:57.791 --> 00:28:59.520
The notion that we all

00:28:59.521 --> 00:29:03.165
identify in different ways
and multiple ways.

00:29:03.166 --> 00:29:06.840
That in fact, I could be
a white woman from the South,

00:29:06.841 --> 00:29:11.675
or I could be a black man
from New York City,

00:29:11.676 --> 00:29:14.310
all different
intersecting identities.

00:29:14.311 --> 00:29:17.000
So, we don't want to leave
you with the notion that

00:29:17.001 --> 00:29:19.700
there's like a shopping list
of things you can do,

00:29:19.701 --> 00:29:21.400
a proper broadening
participation.

00:29:21.401 --> 00:29:23.745
They have to be very
thoughtfully done.

00:29:23.746 --> 00:29:27.450
So, anybody, any thoughts on this

00:29:27.451 --> 00:29:29.610
or think it's
a super important topic

00:29:29.611 --> 00:29:30.620
to just make sure people know

00:29:30.621 --> 00:29:32.050
that word intersectionality,

00:29:32.051 --> 00:29:34.910
and how we can't just assume
all women are the same,

00:29:34.911 --> 00:29:36.010
or all blacks are the same,

00:29:36.011 --> 00:29:38.085
or Latinas are the same.

00:29:38.086 --> 00:29:41.990
&gt;&gt; I guess I would again
stress that as Lucy just said,

00:29:41.991 --> 00:29:44.020
it shouldn't be a checklist
that you should find

00:29:44.021 --> 00:29:46.060
something that you want to do,

00:29:46.061 --> 00:29:48.020
and actually engaging in

00:29:48.021 --> 00:29:49.930
existing structures and what

00:29:49.931 --> 00:29:51.400
people will call scaffolding.

00:29:51.401 --> 00:29:55.170
So, maybe there is a CSTA
that you can engage with.

00:29:55.171 --> 00:29:57.210
Or maybe your department
or your college

00:29:57.211 --> 00:29:59.300
is doing something that
you can engage in.

00:29:59.301 --> 00:30:01.700
It's not like you have to
go out and do a checklist,

00:30:01.701 --> 00:30:03.190
"Oh, I better go do x."

00:30:03.191 --> 00:30:04.800
Find something that's
meaningful for

00:30:04.801 --> 00:30:06.860
you that you can relate
to because that's

00:30:06.861 --> 00:30:09.250
the contribution that
will make a difference to

00:30:09.251 --> 00:30:12.855
people who are on the receiving
end of that contribution.

00:30:12.856 --> 00:30:14.875
&gt;&gt; Any thoughts on that one?

00:30:14.876 --> 00:30:16.770
&gt;&gt; I guess I would add, when

00:30:16.771 --> 00:30:18.060
I'm running a research group,

00:30:18.061 --> 00:30:21.570
one of the things I
try to be aware of is,

00:30:21.571 --> 00:30:23.430
having enough diversity along

00:30:23.431 --> 00:30:25.140
many dimensions that everybody in

00:30:25.141 --> 00:30:27.020
the end can just be themselves,

00:30:27.021 --> 00:30:30.345
it doesn't feel like
a dominant group in a outsider.

00:30:30.346 --> 00:30:31.515
&gt;&gt; Super important.

00:30:31.516 --> 00:30:33.660
&gt;&gt; In the process, you end up

00:30:33.661 --> 00:30:37.850
with a weightlifter who
happens to be female,

00:30:37.851 --> 00:30:43.390
and a quiet Harry Potter male

00:30:43.391 --> 00:30:46.980
and everyone is allowed
to be individuals,

00:30:46.981 --> 00:30:49.040
because there isn't
a dominant culture

00:30:49.041 --> 00:30:52.370
and a recessive or
outsiders feeling.

00:30:52.371 --> 00:30:54.520
&gt;&gt; Again, returning to
the word belonging,

00:30:54.521 --> 00:30:56.380
and I think that's everybody
wants to belong and

00:30:56.381 --> 00:30:58.775
contribute and do
the best work of their lives.

00:30:58.776 --> 00:31:01.350
So, it's super important.

00:31:01.351 --> 00:31:04.790
So, let's do one last question
to each panelists.

00:31:04.791 --> 00:31:08.935
We're starting with
Abalode. One idea,

00:31:08.936 --> 00:31:10.945
Jim already gave us his,
so you get another one.

00:31:10.946 --> 00:31:12.980
&gt;&gt; Oh, wow, I thought I
wasn't going to get one.

00:31:12.981 --> 00:31:14.930
&gt;&gt; Yeah, you're going to
repeat the same one if you

00:31:14.931 --> 00:31:17.195
really want to emphasize that.

00:31:17.196 --> 00:31:20.200
One idea that you can
take out of this room

00:31:20.201 --> 00:31:23.405
today and do immediately,

00:31:23.406 --> 00:31:25.655
that doesn't cost much money.

00:31:25.656 --> 00:31:27.645
For a broadening participation

00:31:27.646 --> 00:31:29.530
as it relates to
innovation and diversity.

00:31:29.531 --> 00:31:30.870
Again, I think you all
really want to come

00:31:30.871 --> 00:31:32.340
back and hit that.

00:31:32.341 --> 00:31:35.830
You know that where we have
the biggest gap in many ways,

00:31:35.831 --> 00:31:38.070
the pipelines is
some sense filling, right?

00:31:38.071 --> 00:31:38.170
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:31:38.171 --> 00:31:40.120
&gt;&gt; We're starting to see more
of these students arrive.

00:31:40.121 --> 00:31:42.820
We want them then in
their research careers,

00:31:42.821 --> 00:31:46.210
or in their corporate careers
to be able to innovate,

00:31:46.211 --> 00:31:47.960
and to create, and to bring

00:31:47.961 --> 00:31:50.140
their best life
experiences to the table,

00:31:50.141 --> 00:31:52.280
and do really great
technology work.

00:31:52.281 --> 00:31:54.585
So, what would be one thing

00:31:54.586 --> 00:31:57.860
that you could leave people
with that they could do?

00:31:57.861 --> 00:31:58.910
&gt;&gt; Can I say two things?

00:31:58.911 --> 00:32:01.120
&gt;&gt; Yes, just like Jim, you
want to be just like Jim?

00:32:01.121 --> 00:32:01.440
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:32:01.441 --> 00:32:02.280
&gt;&gt; Okay, all right.

00:32:02.281 --> 00:32:04.310
&gt;&gt; So, the two things
for me really are,

00:32:04.311 --> 00:32:07.655
be an advocate and
set an example.

00:32:07.656 --> 00:32:09.560
We have an internal
conference here at

00:32:09.561 --> 00:32:11.795
Microsoft called the Whips.

00:32:11.796 --> 00:32:13.110
It's by invitation only.

00:32:13.111 --> 00:32:15.810
It's our top technical
conference and I

00:32:15.811 --> 00:32:19.010
remember there was a few
years ago, Galen Hunt,

00:32:19.011 --> 00:32:22.240
whom some of you, you
may know stood up at

00:32:22.241 --> 00:32:23.995
Whips during the session for

00:32:23.996 --> 00:32:26.995
poster talks and he said,
"Look around the room."

00:32:26.996 --> 00:32:29.680
Everyone looked around
the room and he said,

00:32:29.681 --> 00:32:31.695
"There aren't
very many women here,

00:32:31.696 --> 00:32:34.030
and I think that's a problem and

00:32:34.031 --> 00:32:36.340
so what I would
like you to do is,

00:32:36.341 --> 00:32:39.090
the next time you want
to invite me to Whips,

00:32:39.091 --> 00:32:41.900
take my invitation and give

00:32:41.901 --> 00:32:44.295
it to one of our women
engineers instead."

00:32:44.296 --> 00:32:48.100
And he sat down and I thought
that was really great.

00:32:48.101 --> 00:32:52.265
I think that all of us
have the ability to

00:32:52.266 --> 00:32:56.725
affect decisions that are
made daily in our contexts,

00:32:56.726 --> 00:32:58.730
in a positive direction.

00:32:58.731 --> 00:33:02.330
It's a tool that is relatively
cheap for us to exercise.

00:33:02.331 --> 00:33:03.730
So, that's the first thing

00:33:03.731 --> 00:33:05.590
that I would leave folks with is,

00:33:05.591 --> 00:33:08.695
look at conference selection,

00:33:08.696 --> 00:33:12.310
settings, paper selection
and just be an advocate.

00:33:12.311 --> 00:33:15.175
The second one is,
set an example.

00:33:15.176 --> 00:33:16.800
What I mean by that is that,

00:33:16.801 --> 00:33:18.620
when you are assembling
a team along

00:33:18.621 --> 00:33:21.355
the lines that
Margaret mentioned,

00:33:21.356 --> 00:33:24.385
be intentional by looking

00:33:24.386 --> 00:33:27.420
at the set of people that you
have around you and asking,

00:33:27.421 --> 00:33:30.935
"Am I defaulting to
the same choices?

00:33:30.936 --> 00:33:33.720
Are there people whom I'm
not even considering?

00:33:33.721 --> 00:33:38.590
Is the set of perspectives
that I have actually diverse?"

00:33:38.591 --> 00:33:40.850
The reason to do that is,

00:33:40.851 --> 00:33:43.970
because the statistics
that we're looking at,

00:33:43.971 --> 00:33:45.450
they don't come out of a vacuum.

00:33:45.451 --> 00:33:47.790
They actually come
out of the set of

00:33:47.791 --> 00:33:51.195
people who are in
this room, writ large.

00:33:51.196 --> 00:33:53.930
And the way in which
those statistics change is that,

00:33:53.931 --> 00:33:56.409
all of us exercise

00:33:56.410 --> 00:33:59.850
back pressure against
our default tendencies.

00:33:59.851 --> 00:34:02.760
&gt;&gt; That's absolutely
right. Absolutely right.

00:34:02.761 --> 00:34:04.955
&gt;&gt; I guess I have
to do two now too.

00:34:04.956 --> 00:34:05.890
&gt;&gt; The pressure is on.

00:34:05.891 --> 00:34:06.520
&gt;&gt; I like to keep up.

00:34:06.521 --> 00:34:06.990
&gt;&gt; I get it.

00:34:06.991 --> 00:34:09.570
&gt;&gt; Right. So, one that's

00:34:09.571 --> 00:34:13.360
super cheap and it just
requires you to remember is,

00:34:13.361 --> 00:34:15.380
I think we lose track

00:34:15.381 --> 00:34:17.700
of how much students
and people junior to

00:34:17.701 --> 00:34:22.335
us responds to even modest
tiny bits of feedback.

00:34:22.336 --> 00:34:25.230
So, the quick email at
the end of the class,

00:34:25.231 --> 00:34:26.725
or after a mid-term,

00:34:26.726 --> 00:34:29.740
that says, "You would be

00:34:29.741 --> 00:34:32.670
great if you continued
on in this field.

00:34:32.671 --> 00:34:35.530
You would be great if you
considered grad school."

00:34:35.531 --> 00:34:38.835
It's a 10-word email and it

00:34:38.836 --> 00:34:42.260
will make a difference
in someone's life often.

00:34:42.261 --> 00:34:43.740
Even if they don't do

00:34:43.741 --> 00:34:45.640
what do you think
they'd be great at,

00:34:45.641 --> 00:34:48.220
they've heard it I
guarantee you. That's one.

00:34:48.221 --> 00:34:51.535
The second thing is that
by virtue of who we are,

00:34:51.536 --> 00:34:55.470
we're fairly senior and so we're

00:34:55.471 --> 00:34:56.960
more often in the position of

00:34:56.961 --> 00:34:59.000
being allies to things
that are going on,

00:34:59.001 --> 00:35:01.690
than being in the

00:35:01.691 --> 00:35:04.725
picked on situations
ourselves these days.

00:35:04.726 --> 00:35:07.890
So, I would encourage you
to look at the world with

00:35:07.891 --> 00:35:11.855
your ally hat on and
remember to say things like,

00:35:11.856 --> 00:35:13.575
"I didn't find that funny,

00:35:13.576 --> 00:35:15.340
we don't do that here," you

00:35:15.341 --> 00:35:17.080
don't have to be
completely humorless,

00:35:17.081 --> 00:35:19.150
but you can be the grownup in

00:35:19.151 --> 00:35:21.960
the room that just
raises the bar,

00:35:21.961 --> 00:35:23.950
don't wait for the
picked on person to

00:35:23.951 --> 00:35:26.385
have to say it themselves. I
think those would be my two.

00:35:26.386 --> 00:35:28.590
&gt;&gt; Very important. All right,
Jim, you only get one.

00:35:28.591 --> 00:35:30.600
&gt;&gt; Well, those were
really good answers

00:35:30.601 --> 00:35:32.190
and actually I note that,

00:35:32.191 --> 00:35:35.510
it's all about people and
their individual connections.

00:35:35.511 --> 00:35:37.850
I think when I was
listening to Abalode,

00:35:37.851 --> 00:35:40.225
I thought, "Oh, that's
exactly my answer."

00:35:40.226 --> 00:35:41.750
So, my answer is the same as his.

00:35:41.751 --> 00:35:43.770
I'd use two different words
and that would

00:35:43.771 --> 00:35:45.840
be a mentor and be a role model.

00:35:45.841 --> 00:35:47.965
Right? As Margaret said,

00:35:47.966 --> 00:35:50.760
just reaching out to
somebody and talking to

00:35:50.761 --> 00:35:53.410
them and helping and
that could be a student,

00:35:53.411 --> 00:35:54.529
it could be a colleague,

00:35:54.530 --> 00:35:55.670
it could be a staff, it could be

00:35:55.671 --> 00:35:57.220
lots of different people.

00:35:57.221 --> 00:35:59.505
That personal touch really makes

00:35:59.506 --> 00:36:03.125
a huge difference and to
be a role model as well,

00:36:03.126 --> 00:36:06.720
and I think people
as Margaret said,

00:36:06.721 --> 00:36:08.395
many people here
are senior people,

00:36:08.396 --> 00:36:10.330
look to us and how we behave

00:36:10.331 --> 00:36:12.540
and I think that's
really important.

00:36:12.541 --> 00:36:14.470
There are lots of different ways
to be a role model,

00:36:14.471 --> 00:36:16.280
and being a mentor and
being a role model

00:36:16.281 --> 00:36:19.390
is absolutely no cost at all.

00:36:19.600 --> 00:36:23.785
&gt;&gt; So, I guess I get
two. I'll take two.

00:36:23.786 --> 00:36:26.860
One would be become- this
sounds like a really simple

00:36:26.861 --> 00:36:29.840
one but you'd be surprised
how often it's not done.

00:36:29.841 --> 00:36:32.530
Use the spirit of
inquiry that we all

00:36:32.531 --> 00:36:35.610
have to challenge
things in a nice way.

00:36:35.611 --> 00:36:36.870
So, it's like a question.

00:36:36.871 --> 00:36:38.375
So, when somebody says,

00:36:38.376 --> 00:36:40.460
"I don't think Lucy is qualified

00:36:40.461 --> 00:36:42.470
for that award," spirit
of inquiry will say,

00:36:42.471 --> 00:36:44.345
"Well, what makes
you think that?"

00:36:44.346 --> 00:36:46.230
Or, "I don't think

00:36:46.231 --> 00:36:48.680
that Abalode is a
natural born leader."

00:36:48.681 --> 00:36:51.640
Well, what's that mean anyway?
What do you mean by that?

00:36:51.641 --> 00:36:54.545
One more question, not to say,

00:36:54.546 --> 00:36:58.820
"Oh, you're wrong," or argue
that can come later, right?

00:36:58.821 --> 00:37:00.570
But just a question in

00:37:00.571 --> 00:37:03.340
that learning moment to
find out what do they mean?

00:37:03.341 --> 00:37:05.610
Because we say things
all the time and we have

00:37:05.611 --> 00:37:08.130
no idea what they mean,

00:37:08.131 --> 00:37:10.180
and it's a learning moment
for both people.

00:37:10.181 --> 00:37:11.570
Because it could be that,

00:37:11.571 --> 00:37:13.270
Lucy is not a natural
born leader, right?

00:37:13.271 --> 00:37:14.480
I mean, that might well be.

00:37:14.481 --> 00:37:16.130
By the way I don't think

00:37:16.131 --> 00:37:18.795
there is anything as a natural
born leader, but anyway.

00:37:18.796 --> 00:37:23.080
So, the spirit of inquiry
is underrated and all of us

00:37:23.081 --> 00:37:24.860
are researchers and
innovators and we know

00:37:24.861 --> 00:37:26.640
how to do that and
it's just a question.

00:37:26.641 --> 00:37:28.525
It's the same question you
would ask technically,

00:37:28.526 --> 00:37:29.965
"Why do you think that works?"

00:37:29.966 --> 00:37:32.790
Or whatever. So,
the spirit of inquiry

00:37:32.791 --> 00:37:36.290
I think is super important.

00:37:36.291 --> 00:37:38.740
The other thing I want to say is,

00:37:38.741 --> 00:37:39.800
you don't have to do this alone.

00:37:39.801 --> 00:37:42.745
There are a lot of free resources
and programs out there.

00:37:42.746 --> 00:37:44.500
There are some programs
that cost a little money,

00:37:44.501 --> 00:37:46.805
but lots of ways to be involved
and see what has them.

00:37:46.806 --> 00:37:51.695
CRAW has them, ACMW has programs.

00:37:51.696 --> 00:37:54.090
There are a lot of
great programs out there to

00:37:54.091 --> 00:37:56.440
give you research
based information.

00:37:56.441 --> 00:38:00.550
So, that's the last idea
here is, go become informed.

00:38:00.551 --> 00:38:02.320
This is not a heavy lift for all

00:38:02.321 --> 00:38:04.320
of you smart people to go and

00:38:04.321 --> 00:38:06.480
become informed about what
social science research

00:38:06.481 --> 00:38:08.980
says about under representation.

00:38:08.981 --> 00:38:11.320
Then you're going to be well

00:38:11.321 --> 00:38:13.425
equipped to use
your spirit of inquiry.

00:38:13.426 --> 00:38:15.060
If you don't know what

00:38:15.061 --> 00:38:17.610
unconscious bias means from
a research perspective,

00:38:17.611 --> 00:38:21.140
stereotype threat, civility,
collective intelligence,

00:38:21.141 --> 00:38:22.330
inter-sectionality,

00:38:22.331 --> 00:38:25.040
they're like 10 words you
can find out very easily

00:38:25.041 --> 00:38:26.880
and then you'll be
very well equipped

00:38:26.881 --> 00:38:29.545
to base your actions on research.

00:38:29.546 --> 00:38:32.990
So, those are our ideas

00:38:32.991 --> 00:38:35.510
and I think we've got
a few minutes for questions,

00:38:35.511 --> 00:38:39.930
Q and A if people have
any questions from the audience.

00:38:39.931 --> 00:38:41.710
I see somebody with
their hand up there.

00:38:41.711 --> 00:38:45.240
Wait a minute, someone is
coming to you, I believe.

00:38:51.700 --> 00:38:54.190
&gt;&gt; Thank you all
for your comments.

00:38:54.191 --> 00:38:56.995
The one thing that I didn't
hear talked about was,

00:38:56.996 --> 00:38:59.720
diversity inclusion with
respect to disability,

00:38:59.721 --> 00:39:01.540
and I was wondering if
you would just take

00:39:01.541 --> 00:39:03.625
a minute to address that

00:39:03.626 --> 00:39:09.140
and how we can aid in
DNI for disability.

00:39:09.450 --> 00:39:12.070
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all,

00:39:12.071 --> 00:39:15.025
right down the road you'd
have there Richard Ladner-

00:39:15.026 --> 00:39:16.540
&gt;&gt; Access Computing, right.

00:39:16.541 --> 00:39:17.000
&gt;&gt; -who runs Access Computing.

00:39:17.001 --> 00:39:17.940
Yes. Do you want to
say more about that?

00:39:17.941 --> 00:39:19.000
&gt;&gt; I will just say,

00:39:19.001 --> 00:39:23.040
one of the seven
BPC activities is,

00:39:23.041 --> 00:39:24.650
Access Computing,
which really aimes

00:39:24.651 --> 00:39:26.995
specifically at exactly that.

00:39:26.996 --> 00:39:29.100
As Lucy mentioned
that's been led for

00:39:29.101 --> 00:39:31.050
quite a long time
by Richard Ladner,

00:39:31.051 --> 00:39:33.030
he is an amazing guy at UW.

00:39:33.031 --> 00:39:34.790
&gt;&gt; The other thing
I'll say too is,

00:39:34.791 --> 00:39:36.640
we just discovered
a gap in working with

00:39:36.641 --> 00:39:39.730
Richard and a researcher
Maya Israel,

00:39:39.731 --> 00:39:42.400
who looks a lot at
Universal Design.

00:39:42.401 --> 00:39:44.100
And have discovered

00:39:44.101 --> 00:39:46.095
a pretty big gap
right now in terms of

00:39:46.096 --> 00:39:49.970
computing curriculum
and Universal Design

00:39:49.971 --> 00:39:52.075
for students with
learning disabilities.

00:39:52.076 --> 00:39:55.210
So, Richard works quite a bit
on physical disabilities

00:39:55.211 --> 00:39:58.469
and he's starting to look
at learning disabilities,

00:39:58.470 --> 00:40:00.760
and so that's a thing
to think about.

00:40:00.761 --> 00:40:02.570
Like, how could we
possibly create

00:40:02.571 --> 00:40:04.695
curriculum homework
assignments et cetera,

00:40:04.696 --> 00:40:06.980
that enables students with

00:40:06.981 --> 00:40:08.170
different learning
disabilities to

00:40:08.171 --> 00:40:10.445
succeed in our classrooms.

00:40:10.446 --> 00:40:13.250
&gt;&gt; I'll just add, so CRAW has for

00:40:13.251 --> 00:40:16.210
over 15 years offered
a Grad Cohort for women.

00:40:16.211 --> 00:40:21.560
But a year ago we offered
the first URMD Grad Cohort,

00:40:21.561 --> 00:40:23.400
which was a Co-ed Grad Cohort for

00:40:23.401 --> 00:40:27.130
under-represented minorities
and people with disabilities.

00:40:27.131 --> 00:40:29.075
We just set the date
and the place,

00:40:29.076 --> 00:40:30.955
it's going to be in
March keep an eye out,

00:40:30.956 --> 00:40:35.200
March 2019 will be the second
of those URMD Grad Cohorts.

00:40:35.201 --> 00:40:37.090
&gt;&gt; So, now, I may have understood

00:40:37.091 --> 00:40:39.805
your question or comment
a little bit differently.

00:40:39.806 --> 00:40:41.630
The way I understood
it in connection

00:40:41.631 --> 00:40:44.090
with the diversity and

00:40:44.091 --> 00:40:46.330
its impact on innovation is

00:40:46.331 --> 00:40:48.990
bringing in people with
different abilities-

00:40:48.991 --> 00:40:49.430
&gt;&gt; Good point.

00:40:49.431 --> 00:40:51.365
&gt;&gt; -into the creative process,

00:40:51.366 --> 00:40:53.080
into the idea generation in

00:40:53.081 --> 00:40:56.250
the innovation process and I
think it's absolutely key.

00:40:56.251 --> 00:40:59.220
For the same reasons that
we've talked about that,

00:40:59.221 --> 00:41:03.000
what you get at the end,
is born accessible.

00:41:03.001 --> 00:41:05.480
It's born with
multiple perspective,

00:41:05.481 --> 00:41:08.090
it's born with the ability
to function and be

00:41:08.091 --> 00:41:10.080
useful for a broader range

00:41:10.081 --> 00:41:12.580
of people in a broader
range of contexts.

00:41:12.581 --> 00:41:16.320
So, thank you so much
for bringing that up.

00:41:16.321 --> 00:41:19.050
&gt;&gt; Great point, yes. Anybody
else have a question?

00:41:20.380 --> 00:41:25.320
We deal with? Well, all right,

00:41:25.321 --> 00:41:26.960
so thank you panelists.

00:41:26.961 --> 00:41:31.570
Let's thank our
panelists for yay.

00:41:31.571 --> 00:41:33.280
&gt;&gt; For yay.

00:41:33.281 --> 00:41:36.410
&gt;&gt; For yay. All
right, thank you all.

